N-of-1 and aggregated N-of-1 designs, in which each participant is
repeatedly crossed between an active treatment and a comparator so that
the within-participant contrast estimates that participant's own
treatment effect, are being adopted with increasing frequency across
clinical and behavioral research [@duan2013; @schork2015]. Systematic
and scoping reviews document this growth in general medicine
[@punja2016; @samuel2022], in neurology [@stunnenberg2022], in
psychiatry and clinical psychology [@smith2012; @margolis2019], in
palliative care [@nikles2011], and in translational nutrition
[@schork2017nutrition], and the design now has both a CONSORT reporting
extension and a national policy agenda [@vohra2016cent;
@davidson2018nam]. The principal advantage of these designs over a
parallel-group alternative is efficiency: because each participant
serves as their own control, the between-participant variance component
is removed from the treatment contrast, so pooling a modest number of
individual series in a mixed model yields population-level inference at
sample sizes far below those a parallel-group trial would require
[@zucker1997; @zucker2010; @senn2016mastering]. This efficiency gain
has been quantified directly, in simulation-based comparisons of
aggregated N-of-1 trials against parallel and crossover designs
[@blackston2019; @wang2019] and in a developed literature on
sample-size and power calculation for the N-of-1 setting, including the
serial correlation that repeated within-participant measurement induces
[@senn2019samplesize; @araujo2016; @tang2020; @tueller2022].
Increasingly the target of inference in these designs is not the
average treatment effect but a biomarker-treatment interaction, that
is, whether a baseline marker identifies which participants respond
[@kent2020path; @simon2010; @freidlin2014; @haller2018]. The repeated
within-participant measurement that makes N-of-1 designs efficient for
main effects makes them particularly attractive for this purpose, since
interaction tests are notoriously underpowered in parallel-group
trials, and aggregated N-of-1 designs have accordingly been proposed
and evaluated specifically as a vehicle for predictive-biomarker
validation [@hendrickson2020; @schork2022; @schork2023precision].

